Total: 5.6/10. Shortlist: no. Treaty is the right shape for Switch, but nothing is built, so judges have nothing to run.

I could not check the switch-core citations against the Switch docs (`addressing.py:299`, the linked-rooms endpoint, `register-known`), because that tool was not permitted in this session.

Scores
- Use of Switch: 7/10. Linked rooms per team, owner-scoped addressing and a referee in the room fit Switch well. But the census, plan and removal gate would work the same without Switch.
- Technical execution: 2/10. Every row in the Status table says "not built" or "not run", and the Switch integration (K1) has never been tried.
- Creativity and impact: 7/10. It is clearly different from the many code-review bots, and the problem is real. But the plan itself admits it has no cost figure and that public discussion is quiet.
- Presentation and documentation: 6/10. Limits and status are stated honestly, but there is no video yet and the problem evidence is thin.

Weighted: 0.3×7 + 0.25×2 + 0.25×7 + 0.2×6 = 2.1 + 0.5 + 1.75 + 1.2 = 5.55, rounded to 5.6.

Shortlist: no. A plan with zero working paths loses to dozens of entries that run, however well the idea fits Switch.

Costliest weaknesses
1. Nothing runs. Fix: build only the K1 path first. Pin switch-core 0.31.0, run three agents in linked rooms on a local server, and make `make demo-solo` a single command with seeded repos and a log file. Run `make test` in CI and record one unedited demo.
2. The hero moment is staged. The author plants `finance-nightly` in a log the author also writes, so the census "discovers" what it was built to find. Fix: run the census on data the team did not write, such as public clients of a public OpenAPI spec found by code search. Report misses and false positives too.
3. The isolation claim is in the wrong layer. Addressing policy limits who can talk to an agent, not which repos it can read. A judge from SandboxAQ will notice. Fix: say that repo isolation comes from per-agent credentials. Then demonstrate what Switch does enforce: a refused cross-team address, and private-room text that does not cross the link. Also make consumer PRs wait for the owner's approval.

Claims I doubt
- "Under 3% of those reach clients" misreads Xavier et al. The paper reports that 2.54% of clients are affected, not a share of breaking changes. That figure also weakens the case for the problem.
- `treaty replay` cannot show "what a registry-only list would have missed". Public repos have no gateway logs and no `treaty.yaml`, so the comparison has no data behind it.
- `can-i-remove` uses traffic per API version. If the rename happens inside one version, that traffic cannot show whether anyone still reads the old field.
- "Signs the plan's hash" suggests a cryptographic signature. If it is only a chat message, call it an approval.
